Good. The arch target includes `${NUTTX_CHIP_ABS_DIR}` (the stm32n6 src dir, resolved via symlink) as PUBLIC include dirs, exposed to libboard. So when board src files (libboard) include "stm32n6_gpio.h", it resolves to the arch src dir. And when board.h (in board/include) uses GPIO macros, they resolve at the point of use in .c files that include stm32n6_gpio.h.

So my approach:
1. **board.h**: define `GPIO_LED0`, `GPIO_LED1`, `BOARD_NLEDS` using the GPIO_* macros. These will only be expanded inside stm32n6_userleds.c which includes "stm32n6_gpio.h" first. This follows the NuttX convention (like nucleo-f446re board.h defines GPIO_LD2 and stm32_userleds.c includes "stm32.h" which brings the GPIO macros).

Actually, to be extra safe and avoid the exact same class of latent bug as 参考工程's GPIO_USART1_TX_1, let me have stm32n6_userleds.c include "stm32n6_gpio.h" BEFORE <arch/board/board.h>. That guarantees the macros are defined when board.h's GPIO_LED macros are expanded.

Hmm wait, but there's a subtlety — the include order in the .c file. If I write:
```c
#include <nuttx/config.h>
#include <arch/board/board.h>   /* needs GPIO_* macros */
#include "stm32n6_gpio.h"
```
Then board.h's GPIO_LED0 macro is defined as text (not expanded at include time — macros are expanded at USE time, not definition time). So the order doesn't matter for macro definitions! Macros like `#define GPIO_LED0 (GPIO_MODE_OUTPUT | ...)` are only expanded when GPIO_LED0 is used in code. As long as stm32n6_gpio.h is included before the point of use in stm32n6_userleds.c, it works regardless of header order. 

So the only risk is if some OTHER file (that doesn't include stm32n6_gpio.h) expands GPIO_LED0/GPIO_LED1. That won't happen in our change (only stm32n6_userleds.c uses them).

OK. Now let me also decide: should board.h define the LED macros or should I keep it simpler? Following the nucleo convention, define in board.h. That's the "right" NuttX way and useful for future board code.

